the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f229d07-8a86-5591-a00d-4cf4336bfb79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176398 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a6985012edbaf28ca42d88fd47e3769fa53704cad8f8c09021c944d966a3d48.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176700 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176700 #edb/ref 1061 32915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176700 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c1fad9c-f64b-5dd4-8cbd-7bd0a658b356" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176700 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176701 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176701 #edb/ref 1061 32916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176701 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4111c6f-280b-5d3d-9c92-2a7689b6434a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176701 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176831 #edb/ref 1061 36564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "382a508a-8216-55b4-ab13-c25fd47ad39b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176885 #edb/ref 1061 44590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1123a203-449e-57d5-b756-95e94c17a5b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176886 #edb/ref 1061 44591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e26990d-5383-595c-9264-f4683700a33e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177049 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for a point $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ in spherical polar coordinates,\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWhen we fix one of these coordinates, we obtain some simple surfaces. Let's describe them:\n\n- First, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\rho = c,\n$$\n\nfor $c \\gt 0.$ This equation describes the set of points on a surface whose distance from the origin equals $c.$ So, we obtain a sphere of radius $c$ centered at $O.$ \n\n![](images/1a/1aaecd51b8699f9a537496fa3bd581e153360eec3ef0e4bff4748c9ba3254a55.png)\n\n- Next, consider the equation \n\n$$\n\\theta=c,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c\\in [0,2\\pi).$ This equation describes the set of points that make an angle of $c$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we obtain a *half-plane* whose boundary is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/5a/5a4e9d6481978b148ae696b73e75859808095856aacb269a2069dd3265be8684.png)\n\n- Finally, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\phi = c,\n$$\n\nwhere $c\\in (0,\\pi).$ This equation describes the set of points that make an angle of $c$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. Therefore, we obtain a *half-cone* whose axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/cc/ccae1dd448e3e47ac9488f46302122c140c5efef3bd598b3eabd2ec00ded0f08.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177049 #edb/ref 1065 5990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177049 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cb9503c8-e1b8-5345-8876-3dc47eb3cc7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177049 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236059 #edb/ref 1080 "\\rho =2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236059 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236059 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25770517-4a8b-58a6-aebe-d00c58c307b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236276 #edb/ref 1080 "\\rho =2ϕ" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236276 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236276 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47637a16-4f57-53c6-a381-c5183d8c2b11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236338 #edb/ref 1080 "ϕ=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236338 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236338 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "524c73d2-e407-5ae0-804d-e9a42101c4ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236924 #edb/ref 1080 "\\theta =2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236924 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186236924 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae33c79f-03db-5f7d-9aca-c1041a750e5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237394 #edb/ref 1080 "\\rho =2\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f99c92dd-efb3-508e-8067-3a77809174c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186236059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237472 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1aa2e7ca-c416-5346-a8a6-bdd2a228a74a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266224 