 17592186045417] [17592186269015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269015 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/db/db4700a08fd81ab6d2bc07d80d72fb1fc9edea6c0cdf652b251a755b53a2b148.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48cc5ea4-9288-5e4d-b708-8718d21e8b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269015 #edb/ref 1074 "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $3.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269021 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269021 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269021 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269021 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c6c5a6f-f486-57c2-a5c5-e573d7067374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269021 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](images/37/3775fdcb48fd015097b484b6d5ebaefcecd4ef10298cb18b34226a8eae1be1ef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135302" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269022 #edb/ref 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6e0d64d-6adc-551a-b870-a3578b510cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269022 #edb/ref 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/00/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269